# What `aipl report` prints for the smallest program there is, written down as the
# case's `--- report ---` section — so this file is both the program and what a
# report of it looks like, and `check` holds the two together.
#
# An empty `main` allocates nothing and executes three instructions; the whole of
# its object is the one function, `main`, and the rest of the object's size is the
# symbol table and headers around it.

fn main() {}
--- report ---
allocations       0
deallocations     0
reallocations     0
bytes allocated   0
peak live bytes   0
instructions      3
binary size       488 bytes (code 8, data 1, metadata 479)

function      calls  code bytes
main              1           8
